<<<
obelus. PI. -li (-i).
===
**obelus.** Pl. *-li* (-ī).
>>>

<<<
obfuscate makes -cable, -tor;

see -ABLE 1, -OR.
===
**obfuscate** makes *-cable*, *-tor*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1, ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
object, vb. 1. O. makes objector; see -OR. 2. France rightly objects to allow Germany to assume a position
===
**object,** vb. 1. *O.* makes *objector*; see ^^-OR^^. 2. *France rightly objects* to allow *Germany to assume a position*
>>>

<<<
+ in Morocco which .../Uhe cabdrivers object to pay their proportion of the increase.
===
+ *in Morocco which .../The cabdrivers object to pay their proportion of the increase.*
>>>

<<<
To allow, or to allowing? to pay, or to paying? The infinitive is deprecated & the gerund recommended; for this see GERUND 3. objectify. For inflexions see VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE, 6. objection. They have been blocked by the objections of farmers & landlords to provide suitable land. Or to providing? see GERUND 3.
===
*To allow*, or *to allowing*? *to pay*, or *to paying*? The infinitive is deprecated & the gerund recommended; for this see ^^GERUND^^ 3.

**objectify.** For inflexions see ^^VERBS IN -IE, -Y, -YE^^, 6.

**objection.** *They have been blocked by the objections of farmers & landlords* to provide *suitable land.* Or *to providing*? see ^^GERUND^^ 3.
>>>

<<<
objective genitive. See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**objective genitive.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
QBJECT-SHUFFLING. The conferring of a name on a type of mistake, making it recognizable & avoidable, is worth while if the mistake is common.
===
**OBJECT-SHUFFLING.** The conferring of a name on a type of mistake, making it recognizable & avoidable, is worth while if the mistake is common.
>>>

<<<
Object-shuffling describes what unwary writers are apt to do
===
***Object-shuffling*** describes what unwary writers are apt to do
>>>

<<<
You can inspire courage in a person, or inspire a person with courage; the change of construction is objectshuffling, which, with the verb inspire, is legitimate & does not offend against idiom; but with instil the object-shuffling would be wrong;
===
You can *inspire* courage in a person, or *inspire* a person with courage; the change of construction is object-shuffling, which, with the verb *inspire*, is legitimate & does not offend against idiom; but with *instil* the object-shuffling would be wrong;
>>>

<<<
you can instil courage into a person; to instil a person with courage is contrary to idiom.
===
you can *instil* courage into a person; to *instil* a person with courage is contrary to idiom.
>>>

<<<
beginning with in-, sub-, pre-, &c.
===
beginning with *in-*, *sub-*, *pre-*, &c.
>>>

<<<
the verbal parts of substituo & instillo & praefigo mean to put
===
the verbal parts of *substituo* & *instillo* & *praefigo* mean to put
>>>

